            <ArticleTitle>Pain in cognitively impaired, non-communicating children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>460-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To detail the everyday occurrence of pain in non-communicating children with cognitive impairment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty four parents of cognitively impaired verbally non-communicating children completed pain diaries over a two week period. Each day, for five defined periods, parents rated whether their child had been in pain, and if so, its severity and duration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty five (73.5%) children experienced pain on at least one day, with moderate or severe levels of pain being experienced by 23 (67.6%). Four children (11.7%) experienced moderate or severe pain lasting longer than 30 minutes on five or more days. No child was receiving active pain management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Everyday pain in children with severe cognitive impairment is common, yet is rarely actively treated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
